// Practice quiz: Scope and closures
// First draft: unreviewed. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: html-css-js/javascript-fundamentals/scope-and-closures

::o1 block scope::A const is declared inside an if block. A line after that block reads it. What happens? {
~It reads the value, because const is function-scoped #Not quite. That describes var, not const.
~It reads undefined #Not quite. Reading an undeclared name throws rather than giving undefined.
=A ReferenceError, because the const is scoped to the block #Correct. Block-scoped names are visible only within their braces.
~A SyntaxError before anything runs #Not quite. This is a run-time lookup failure.
}

::o1 lookup direction::Which statement describes scope lookup? {
~A line can read names in any scope in the file #Not quite. Inner scopes are not visible from outside.
~A line can read names only in its own block #Not quite. Enclosing scopes are visible too.
~Lookups go inward first, then outward #Not quite. They never go inward.
=A line can read names in enclosing scopes, but not names inside nested scopes #Correct. Lookups go outward only.
}

::o2 closure state::A factory function declares let n \= 0 and returns a function that increases n and returns it. Two functions are made from separate factory calls. Each is called twice. What does the last call of the second one return? {
~4 #Not quite. That would mean they share one n.
~1 #Not quite. n is kept between calls, so the second call gives 2.
=2 #Correct. Each factory call creates its own n.
~undefined #Not quite. The function returns n.
}

::o2 private state::What makes a closure variable private? {
~The const keyword #Not quite. Privacy comes from scope, not from the keyword.
=Only functions created in that scope can reach it #Correct. Outside code has no name for it.
~A private keyword in JavaScript functions #Not quite. No such keyword applies to local variables.
~Naming it with a leading underscore #Not quite. A naming convention enforces nothing.
}

::o3 loop with let::A for loop with let i from 0 to 1 pushes a function returning i into an array. After the loop you call both functions. What do they return? {
=0 and 1 #Correct. Each pass has its own binding of i.
~2 and 2 #Not quite. That is the shared-variable result you get from var.
~1 and 1 #Not quite. That assumes one shared binding, stopping at the last pass.
~undefined and undefined #Not quite. The bindings stay alive inside the closures.
}
